-- Consent rows for the 30 brands on the consented list that have a handle.
--
-- This is the switch that starts the scraper: the instagram-scrape cron (41 5 * * *,
-- instagram_scrape_dispatch()) visits every subject whose consent_status is
-- 'granted', so these rows cause their posts to be copied into the public
-- instagram-posts bucket on the next run.
--
-- consent_source is 'other' and the note says exactly where the permission came
-- from and what is missing from it, following the five rows already here. It is a
-- record of an operator attestation, not of a signed agreement, and it says so --
-- so that if a brand ever asks, the answer is in the row.
--
-- consent_by is set to 17 (Raskin, the account that made the attestation). The
-- five existing rows leave it null and name him in the note instead; the column
-- exists for this, so it is filled.
--
-- HiCOLOR (388) is deliberately absent. Its stored handle is 'vireohealth', the
-- parent company's account, so consenting it would copy Vireo Health's posts onto
-- HiColor's page. It needs its own handle first.
ON CONFLICT (subject_type, subject_id) DO NOTHING;

SELECT count(*) AS consent_rows,
       count(*) FILTER (WHERE consent_status='granted') AS granted
  FROM public.instagram_consent;
